WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.199
Interviewer: How have you been surprised by
evolution?

00:00:02.199 --> 00:00:05.970
Richard Lenski: Well, I think one of the things
that's really surprised me is just how long

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.459
this experiment's gone on.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.459
When I originally
started it, I expected it to run for maybe

00:00:10.459 --> 00:00:15.119
a year or longer. And now here we are, almost
30 years later. But within the experiment

00:00:15.119 --> 00:00:20.200
itself there have been a lot of surprises.
One of the most exciting to me has been the

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.960
fact that we see that even after all this
time in a constant environment, the bacteria

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.050
are continuing to evolve and adapt. It's as
though there's really no limit to evolution,

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:35.120
and that's quite different from what I imagined
when I started the experiment. Another thing

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.760
that's gotten a lot of attention is one of
our 12 populations has gone off and evolved

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.280
this new ability to grow on citrate. It's
gone off in this new direction that none of

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:52.070
the other populations have gone and that other
E. coli out in nature are unable to do. So

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.219
those are a couple of the big surprises within
the experiment, and I think that they show

00:00:55.219 --> 00:01:00.550
that when we stop and thing about evolution,
and the billions of years over which it's

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.889
happened and the vast scale of our planet,
evolution is just constantly generating a

00:01:04.889 --> 00:01:06.670
lot of exciting new surprises.

